<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Västergötland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as West Gothland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Västergötland is a historical province in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, between lakes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vanern">Vänern</a> and Vättern and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a> coast. The core land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a>, it had an early bishopric at Skara and its own law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastgotalagen">Västgötalagen</a>. It was joined to the Swedish kingdom around 1000, and much of its coast was long contested with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vastergotland.jpg" alt="Västergötland" width="320" height="729" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lapplänning (highlighting by Lokal_Profil) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sverigekarta-Landskap_V%C3%A4sterg%C3%B6tland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Västergötland is one of the 25 traditional provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, in the south-west of the country. Its north-western border is marked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vanern">Lake Vänern</a>, Sweden's largest lake, and its north-eastern border by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vattern">Lake Vättern</a>, the second largest. The southern and eastern part is hilly, part of the southern Swedish highlands, while the northern and western portions belong to the Central Swedish lowland, the Västgöta plains, which contain hills of Silurian sedimentary rock such as Kinnekulle, Halleberg, Hunneberg and Billingen. The largest river is the Göta älv, which drains Vänern to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>, and along it several important towns have stood for centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There are many ancient remains in the province, among them dolmens of the Funnelbeaker culture near Falköping and Finnestorp, near Larv, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> site of weapon sacrifices. Its people, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a>, appear in the writings of the Greek Ptolemaios as Goutai and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> in the 6th century as Gautigoths.</p><p>Västergötland was the heartland of Götaland, once an independent petty kingdom with a long line of Geatish kings, known mainly from foreign sources and legends. When it became part of the Swedish kingdom is much debated: some date it as early as the 6th century, from the Swedish-Geatish wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>, others as late as the 12th century.</p><p>The province received early influence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> and is generally considered the bridgehead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>'s advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Around 1000 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olof-skotkonung">Olof Skötkonung</a> is held to have been baptised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husaby">Husaby</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vanern">Lake Vänern</a>, and because the new faith met heavy opposition elsewhere in his kingdom he had to restrict Christian activity to Västergötland.</p><p>When the provincial law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastgotalagen">Västgötalagen</a>, was written in the 13th century, the province had 517 churches. The seat of the diocese was apparently first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husaby">Husaby</a>, but from 1150 it was Skara. In the civil wars that followed the election of King Stenkil in the 11th century, Swedish and Geatish dynasties vied for control, and Västergötland was several times independent under kings such as Inge I and Magnus the Strong.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lying along the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, the province was often involved in armed disputes. Battles included Älgarås in 1205, Lena in 1208, Hova in 1275 and Falköping in 1389, and later the Sweden-Danish wars. In 1658 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> annexed the Scanian lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a>, and the last combat on Västergötland soil came in the Scanian War of the 1670s.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>, is its largest city. The province serves no administrative function, and since 1998 nearly all of it lies in Västra Götaland County. Skara Cathedral is the oldest cathedral in the original parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and Läckö Castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vanern">Lake Vänern</a> is regarded as one of the country's finest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> castles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Västergötland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Svear of the Mälaren valley and Uppland, centred on the royal and cult site of Uppsala, appear in sources from Tacitus onward, while the Geats lived in <b>Västergötland</b> and Östergötland to the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…many Swedes joined the Byzantine emperor’s Varangian Guard, formed in the late 10th century, that a law of <b>Västergötland</b> barred anyone staying in “Greece” from inheriting, and Cnut’s conquest brought a royal guard of housecarls to England, which fought for Harold Godwinson at the Battle of Hastings in…</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Erik Knutsson won a crushing victory over the Danish-backed King Sverker II of Sweden, probably near Kungslena in <b>Västergötland</b>, and took the throne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-31">31 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Their heartland lay in <b>Västergötland</b> and Östergötland, around lakes Vänern and Vättern.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-geats">Kingdom of the Geats</a></li></ul></section>
